Title: GOOD MOURNING
Credit: Episode 105
Author: Development draft
Draft date: 6 November 2026
Source: Have You Tried Being Better?
Notes: First draft. Target 9:45 story plus 0:15 credits. The hearing runs in near-real time from 09:48; screen and wall clocks must be reconciled in the edit.

# 105.1 | 00:00-01:15

INT. MUNICIPAL CLINIC ANNEX - HEARING ROOM - MONDAY 09:48

A folding table. An official clock. The chipped kettle on a side table, unplugged beneath a NO HOT APPLIANCES sign.

ADJUDICATOR RAO, 50s, opens an already-filed evidence packet. A CLERK monitors the contract portal.

RAO
This is an interim group-contract review. Not a vote on whether grief is good for us. Renewal locks at ten. Filing alone does not stop it.

Counsel VALE, 40s, immaculate and unhurried, gestures to Martin.

VALE
Mr. Hale chose the Hold. The proposed interruption places patients like him at risk.

MARTIN
I sleep. I go to work. Before it, I couldn't.

Isha looks at him. He expects an argument.

ISHA
I'm glad it helped.

VALE
Then you agree continuity must be protected.

ISHA
His continuity. Not your authority to decide mine.

Rao marks the distinction in the margin.

Through a glazed door, the adjacent clinic corridor is visible. Pell sits by it, apart from the other witnesses. Len's unpaid-shift list is on a distant noticeboard.

# 105.2 | 01:15-02:45

INT. HEARING ROOM - CONTINUOUS

Isha sets two old receipts beside her written statement.

ISHA
I renewed twice. After Mum died. I told everyone I hadn't needed it.

VALE
You accept that the service delivered a benefit.

ISHA
It helped. That is why what you did is worse. You took a thing I could choose and made it the price of keeping my home.

She turns to Martin, not the adjudicator.

ISHA
I'm not asking them to take yours away.

Martin nods once.

Mara takes the witness chair. She does not straighten it.

MARA
These are my release requests. Twelve slots, including two reschedules. All canceled by the provider. No individual clinical reason is recorded.

RAO
And the classification document?

MARA
My design. I approved pressure on accounts delaying renewal. I didn't ask what happened after the notice went out.

RAO
You understand your disclosure can be referred for professional review?

MARA
Yes. It's not submitted on condition that it won't be.

Vale examines the exports. The account-holder source hashes match the lodged copies.

VALE
The data show cancellations. They don't establish an improper clinical policy.

Pell looks toward the clinic corridor. Mara does not look at him.

# 105.3 | 02:45-04:15

INT. HEARING ROOM - CONTINUOUS

Suri lays the original contract beside the digital version.

SURI
Clause eight. Clinical refusal does not end the housing supplement. Nobody's asking you to invent a right.

RAO
The original is accepted for this interim hearing.

LEN
Three pots of money. Clinical care is paid. Disputed administration fees are sealed in escrow. Housing is being bridged from the cooperative reserve.

He holds up Tessa's signed table. Vale receives the same page.

LEN
Five thousand one hundred. Ten days. It isn't care funding and it won't last a month.

VALE
An arrangement the residents freely chose.

SURI
After you stopped the payment we were entitled to receive.

Rao holds up a hand. Suri stops.

LEN
These future license and incentive allocations could pay the transitional care hours. I'm identifying the invoices, not saying the money's already in their account.

RAO
Under the contract's interim-service clause, I can direct a local arrangement pending review. I cannot dissolve WellSpring or order treatment for individuals.

VALE
Director Pell's cancellation authority reflects clinical responsibility. Unless he confirms otherwise, those codes are open to interpretation.

PELL
May I have a moment?

Rao looks at the clock: just after 09:52.

RAO
Ninety seconds. Here or next door. We do not stop the renewal clock.

# 105.4 | 04:15-05:45

INT. ADJACENT CLINIC CORRIDOR - CONTINUOUS

Pell stands at the roster. The clinic continues around him. A cleaner wipes a door handle; a patient waits for transport. No one pauses for his moral crisis.

Len joins him. Removes his own name from VOLUNTARY COVER.

PELL
I didn't ask you to do that shift.

LEN
You circulated an empty roster. We know what the empty spaces mean.

PELL
If everyone withdraws—

LEN
Then someone has to pay for care.

Pell looks at the crossed-out name.

LEN
Dad needs taking home. I can't be the son in your testimonial and the empty chair in his kitchen.

Pell turns toward a framed photograph: the epidemic clinic, twenty-eight years earlier. His younger self carrying a box.

PELL
We had no time to ask all the questions.

LEN
It's been twenty-eight years.

Let the remaining time live. Pell reads the roster one name at a time. The cleaner asks him to move so she can reach the handle behind him. He moves.

The clerk appears at the doorway. Not a warning speech. Just presence.

Pell goes back inside before the ninety seconds expire.

# 105.5 | 05:45-07:15

INT. HEARING ROOM - CONTINUOUS

Pell takes the witness chair.

PELL
That's my authorization. The release slots were removed to protect active-account funding. They were not canceled because of findings specific to Mara's condition.

VALE
Director, the distinction—

PELL
Is the question I've been avoiding.

He turns to the source-label exhibit.

PELL
The archive reconstructions were approved to look like recovered recordings. I approved that description. Some people found them comforting. That does not make the description true.

Rao writes. Doesn't congratulate him.

RAO
The evidence supports an interim stay. Group renewal is paused. Housing supplements are to resume under existing eligibility, without clinical-consent conditions, pending review.

The clerk enters each direction as Rao speaks.

RAO
Clinical appointments remain available by individual choice. Future disputed administration allocations may fund the filed transitional service plan, with paid staffing and an auditable cap. Existing escrow stays untouched. Management conduct is referred separately.

MARA
And the messages?

RAO
No undisclosed reconstruction is to be labeled an original under this contract. Account holders must be offered correction or cancellation. Your mother's recording is not an exhibit beyond your source export.

Rao signs.

RAO
Transmit it. Now.

# 105.6 | 07:15-08:30

INT. HEARING ROOM - CONTINUOUS

The clerk presses SEND. A spinning indicator.

09:55 on the clock.

Nobody gives a victory speech. Tessa, in the public seats, holds the reserve ledger closed with both hands.

The kettle's cable has slipped onto the floor. Isha picks it up so no one trips. Such a small task, suddenly available.

CLERK
Received by the municipal gateway. Waiting for contract acknowledgment.

LEN
Received isn't applied.

CLERK
I know.

Len closes his mouth. Lets the clerk do the job.

Martin checks the time of his actual clinic appointment.

Mara's phone alarm vibrates: CHECK ORDER ACKNOWLEDGMENT. She silences it rather than adding another demand.

The screen changes.

GROUP CONTRACT: STAYED. NEXT RENEWAL: PENDING REVIEW.
HOUSING SUPPLEMENT: REINSTATED. PAYMENT PROCESSING.

The clock reads 09:56.

CLERK
Acknowledged. Four minutes before lock.

Suri lets out a breath. Tessa opens the ledger and writes the acknowledgment number, not a new spending promise.

Pell remains seated. No one moves toward him.

# 105.7 | 08:30-09:45

INT. CLINIC CORRIDOR - MOMENTS LATER

Nadi waits by a private consultation door. Mara comes out carrying the evidence bag. Isha follows a few steps behind.

NADI
I have a cancellation this morning. You can use it for assessment, or we can discuss resuming. You don't have to decide because you won a hearing.

MARA
Do I look like I need it?

NADI
I'd rather ask how you're doing.

Mara can't immediately answer. Isha almost does. Stops herself.

MARA
I haven't slept properly. I keep thinking if I stop doing something, she'll be more dead.

Nadi leaves room around that.

NADI
Then let's talk about tonight. Not the rest of your life.

Isha takes the kettle bag from Mara.

ISHA
I'll be in the canteen.

Not I'll wait outside the door. Not Tell me what you choose.

Mara sits in the chair beside the consultation room. The service chime begins from an unattended screen down the hall.

She looks toward the sound, then back at the open door.

For the first time, the choice is not printed on the wall.

CUT TO BLACK.

[[09:45-10:00 — End credits.]]
